   8.194	{"model":"llama3.2:1b","response":"The History of the Printing Press\n\nThe printing press, invented by Johannes Gutenberg in the 15th century, revolutionized the way people received information and played a crucial role in shaping the course of human history. The printing press was a groundbreaking innovation that allowed for mass production of printed materials, making books and other written works more widely available and accessible to the general public.\n\nThe Early Days of Printing\n\nBefore the invention of the printing press, books were typically copied by hand, a laborious and time-consuming process that involved several stages: lettering, calligraphy, illustration, and copying. The process of copying was carried out by scribes, who worked in a hierarchical structure, with the most skilled scribes at the top, followed by assistants and eventually, apprentices. The scribes were responsible for creating the illustrations and illustrations for the book, which were then copied by hand.\n\nIn the 10th century, the monastery in Ebstorf, Germany, became a center of learning and culture. The monastery was home to a group of scholars who were known for their expertise in Latin and Greek. One of these scholars, Herman of Ebstorf, was a prolific writer who wrote several books on theology, medicine, and philosophy. Herman's writing style was characterized by his use of Latin, which was the language of the time. The monastery's library, which was built in the 10th century, became a hub for the transmission of knowledge.\n\nThe Development of the Printing Press\n\nJohannes Gutenberg, a German goldsmith and printer, is credited with inventing the first movable-type printing press in the 1440s. Gutenberg's innovation was the use of metal type, which allowed for the creation of individual characters for each letter of the alphabet. The metal type was made by casting it in the shape of the letters, which enabled the printer to create a set of characters with identical designs.\n\nGutenberg's printing press was powered by a hand-cranked screw, which drove the press's screw. The screw was connected to a hand-cranked cylinder that was used to press the ink onto the paper. The press was relatively small, measuring only 20 inches in diameter. The press was capable of producing 60 lines of text per minute, which was a significant increase in productivity.\n\nThe Printing Press in the 15th Century\n\nThe printing press was first used in the 1450s in Mainz, Germany. The first book printed using the new technology was a Bible, which was printed in 1455. The Bible was","context":[128000,128006,9125,128007,271,38766,1303,33025,2696,25,6790,220,2366,18,198,15724,2696,25,220,1591,17907,220,2366,21,271,128009,128006,882,128007,271,8144,264,11944,9071,315,520,3325,220,1049,15,4339,389,279,3925,315,279,18991,3577,13,128009,128006,78191,128007,271,791,11346,315,279,51869,8612,271,791,18991,3577,11,36592,555,55205,52686,304,279,220,868,339,9478,11,14110,1534,279,1648,1274,4036,2038,323,6476,264,16996,3560,304,46620,279,3388,315,3823,3925,13,578,18991,3577,574,264,64955,19297,430,5535,369,3148,5788,315,17124,7384,11,3339,6603,323,1023,5439,4375,810,13882,2561,323,15987,311,279,4689,586,382,791,23591,21882,315,51869,271,10438,279,28229,315,279,18991,3577,11,6603,1051,11383,22523,555,1450,11,264,9511,1245,323,892,70840,1920,430,6532,3892,18094,25,6661,287,11,1650,93170,11,40134,11,323,32139,13,578,1920,315,32139,574,11953,704,555,274,56236,11,889,6575,304,264,70994,6070,11,449,279,1455,26611,274,56236,520,279,1948,11,8272,555,57619,323,9778,11,72351,13,578,274,56236,1051,8647,369,6968,279,45543,323,45543,369,279,2363,11,902,1051,1243,22523,555,1450,382,644,279,220,605,339,9478,11,279,85861,304,469,25604,33942,11,10057,11,6244,264,4219,315,6975,323,7829,13,578,85861,574,2162,311,264,1912,315,31839,889,1051,3967,369,872,19248,304,20023,323,18341,13,3861,315,1521,31839,11,61338,315,469,25604,33942,11,574,264,63100,7061,889,6267,3892,6603,389,54641,1